Shared array producer observed while scanning 007-012
  t/haskell-fixtures/Main.hs:prepareArray and arrayDriverSource own the following
  file operations; FixtureSupport.hs owns subprocesses/hashing. Uses built
  thc-fixtures (with its in-process CBD decoder), shared exporter/plugin, GHC
  9.14.1, matching ghc-pkg and installed array-0.5.8.0 plus base/GHC interfaces.
  The selected package DB's array registration is read with ghc-pkg field/describe.
  Native drivers require 64-bit Int. Host byte order and GHC --info enter manifest.
  Each indexed source is exported separately at pre and post-tidy boundaries,
  with its named entry roots and THC_SOURCE_NOTES=true. Each invocation calls
  the shared exporter and rebuilds/checks plugin tools internally.
  A generated driver imports every source module for that family. GHC --make
  -O2 -fforce-recomp -dcore-lint -dstg-lint compiles it with the two fixture include
  directories, then the producer feeds declared cases on stdin and checks row keys.
  Manifests hash every listed CBD, native driver, native executable and oracle,
  plus fixture sources, Main.hs, FixtureSupport.hs, thc.cabal, platform exporter
  scripts and only top-level src/compiler/THC/*.hs. Full tool closure remains
  broader. Existing CBDs are not cleared first; old manifest/obsolete audit/expected
  files are removed. These source receipts do not establish producer edges.
  Windows uses PowerShell and writes stage/index/export.args; native suffix .exe.
  For every stage/index, GHC writes scratch files in stage/index/ghc/.
  Java consumers read manifest, every receipt path, all stage CBDs and oracle TSV;
  Java loads CBD directly. The checked consumers below do not write synthetic CBDs.

